module gunbc.executor_schedule_retention

data schedule_retention_policy_note: String = "The v1 claim executor holds the WHOLE batch schedule before it runs a witness, so per-module retention is EXACT, not heuristic: each module's retained typed state (typed-module result, normalize/ownership diagnostic memos, parse-body entry in the process-shared MultiEntryIndex) is refcounted by the number of remaining scheduled entries whose closure reaches it, and dropped the moment that count hits zero. Derived from declared inputs (the schedule x the module-graph closure); no threshold, no recency heuristic, no GC. DESIGN grounding: S2 (drop dead weight is the master move), S4 (a heuristic is never necessary in a closed system - the schedule exists, so retention is computed not guessed), S5 (fail-closed). Authority: this carrier; the Rust realization in cli_run.rs mirrors each fact below and schedule_retention_policy_matches_modeled_authority reds on drift - the extdeps.realization.resolved_graph exact-byte-cap lockstep pattern applied to a policy. RELEASE AND CAPACITY ARE INDEPENDENT, AND THIS ROW NO LONGER CLAIMS OTHERWISE (corrected 2026-08-07). It previously carried a dissolution trigger reading 'the cross-entry typed-module-memo ladder SpacePacked eviction (PR-beta)' - naming a type that no longer exists, and, worse than the stale name, asserting a supersession that is not true. Exact schedule release answers WHEN a module's retained state is dead: remaining demand reaches zero. A capacity bound answers WHAT HAPPENS while demand is still live and a ceiling is reached: refuse the store, or replace an entry. std.cache_interface.ProviderRetention was split into release_policy x capacity precisely so a carrier can state both, and this file itself already counts released_modules and evicted_modules as separate quantities. So adding a byte ceiling to the typed-module memo does not retire exact remaining-demand release, and nothing here dissolves on it. A later unified realization may replace the Rust implementation; that would be a realization change, not this policy being superseded by a capacity axis."

data schedule_retention_tunable_count: Int = 0

data schedule_retention_tunable_count_note: String = "Zero by construction: the retention rule is the schedule's remaining demand, full stop - no threshold, no recency, no confidence dial. Mirrored by SCHEDULE_RETENTION_TUNABLE_COUNT in cli_run.rs; a nonzero here (a smuggled heuristic - S5's confidence-threshold tell) must move BOTH surfaces and face review."

data schedule_retention_grain_is_per_module: Bool = true

data schedule_retention_heads_stay_resident: Bool = true

data schedule_retention_heads_note: String = "Heads (the whole-pool parse snapshot and the bare/qualified census layers) are NOT per-module state and are never evicted - the #6848 bare-name census requires the whole-pool heads resident for the run's lifetime (heads-only parse #6956/#6972 precedent). Held by construction: only per-module cache keys are recorded for eviction."

data schedule_retention_retain_on_unknown: Bool = true

data schedule_retention_retain_on_unknown_note: String = "When reachability CANNOT be computed for a cached module (a provenance gap), the safe arm is RETAIN - correctness-fail-closed but memory-fail-open - so it is a typed, per-module, COUNTED RetentionUnknown row, never a silent retain-everything (that absorbing fallback is a T-as-ignorance widen whose frequency the corpus would never see, S5)."

data schedule_retention_release_and_eviction_counted_apart: Bool = true

data schedule_retention_release_vs_eviction_note: String = "A module is RELEASED when its retention refcount reaches zero; it is EVICTED only when that release actually dropped recorded cache state. The two are different facts and are counted apart (schedule_releases vs schedule_evictions, released_modules vs evicted_modules). They diverge on every run whose arming reaches modules the run never reconciles - the ordinary case, because arming spans the whole DISCOVERED roster while the affected set resolves a subset. Fusing them overstates the mechanism by that entire population: a live CI receipt read schedule_evictions=2200 against modules_refcounted=2200 and typed_cache_peak=883, i.e. it claimed to have evicted every module it had ever heard of, ~2.5x the modules that were ever cached, and a counter that equals the arm count by construction carries no information at all. Rust lockstep: SCHEDULE_RETENTION_RELEASE_AND_EVICTION_COUNTED_APART in cli_run.rs; discriminating RED: unreconciled_module_releases_without_evicting (an armed-but-never-reconciled module must release without evicting - re-fusing the counters reds it)."

data schedule_retention_entry_line_asserts_execution: Bool = false

data schedule_retention_entry_line_note: String = "The per-entry drain line witnesses ONE fact: the schedule moved PAST that entry, so its per-module state can no longer be read. It asserts nothing about selection or execution - index_arm_schedule_retention walks every DISCOVERED row before any predict-skip, so the line also appears for entries the affected set skipped without ever resolving them. It was formerly spelled entry='...', which two independent readers hours apart took as proof the entry had been scheduled and run, and that misreading cost hours of misdirected diagnosis on a real selection defect. It is now schedule_passed_entry='...' beside an ARMED-line legend, because a label that asserts a fact its mechanism cannot support is a defect of the same family as a wrong number (DESIGN section 5: a diagnostic must be typed and located, and locating it on the wrong subject is not locating it)."

data schedule_retention_underflow_refuses: Bool = true

data schedule_retention_underflow_note: String = "A decrement past zero (a scheduled entry demands state the refcount said was fully consumed) is a schedule-derivation defect and REFUSES, typed and located - the mechanism never serves a wrong verdict against evicted state. Content-key recompute-on-miss is the correctness license; the refusal is the loudness that keeps a derivation bug from hiding."
